  "summary": "Squinting close-ups, Spanish deserts, Morricone screams — the West rewritten as opera.",
  "description": "The spaghetti western — launched internationally by Sergio Leone's A Fistful of Dollars (1964) — recoded the American western: amoral antiheroes in ponchos, extreme close-up squints intercut with wide Almería desert vistas, gallows humor, and Ennio Morricone's music (whips, coyote howls, chanted wah-wah) acting as on-screen color. Cinematographers like Tonino Delli Colli favored harsh sun, dust filters and unstylish sweat; production design turned Almería's Tabernas desert into a mythic Mexico. The style's grammar — the three-way standoff, the torture-of-the-hero, the dressed-down costume — spread through hundreds of Eurowesterns and redefined the genre's visual cruelty and cool.",
  "culturalContext": "The spaghetti western rewrote the American West as Italian opera: Leone's squint-and-standoff grammar, Morricone's sound design and Almería's deserts redefined genre cool, and its influence runs through every antihero western from Tarantino to global genre cinema.",
